WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:09.700
Most PDFs on the web are inaccessible - making
it so people using assistive technologies

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.620
can’t navigate or read them.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:16.029
In this video, we'll start with what PDFs
need to be accessible.

00:00:16.029 --> 00:00:22.010
Then, we’ll manually test an inaccessible
PDF to see how it affects assistive technology

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:23.080
users.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:28.400
We'll end with why we prefer HTML webpages
to PDFs.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:35.370
Like HTML webpages, PDFs need alternative
text, correct heading use, the right color

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:38.030
contrast, and other accessibility design techniques.

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:43.329
But, PDFs also need to be tagged and have
their reading order checked.

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:48.640
Tagging tells screen readers what content
is headings, paragraph text, images, and links.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:55.410
And, the reading order makes sure the content
is read in, you guessed it, the right order.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:01:00.360
Most PDFs don't have the right tagging or
reading order, which means they are unreadable

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.199
to someone using assistive technology like
a screen reader.

00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:09.460
Let's take a look at how inaccessible PDFs
would affect someone using a screen reader.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:14.970
I'm going to use Mac's VoiceOver screen reader,
and the PDF is opened in Adobe Acrobat.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:19.880
Where the PDF is viewed and the screen reader
settings can affect the experience.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:24.400
If you want to try testing your own PDFs,
check out the links for Mac's VoiceOver screen

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.580
reader and Window's NVDA screen reader below
- both are free.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:31.280
Okay, let's get to this example.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:35.349
This PDF was saved using Print and then Save
as PDF.

00:01:35.349 --> 00:01:40.720
When saving a PDF this way, it can make it
so the PDF is one giant image.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:43.180
The screen reader won’t be able to read
it at all.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.260
Let’s hear what this sounds like.

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:46.280
VoiceOver: Window.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.410
Alert, document unavailable pane.

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:52.520
VoiceOver: Alert, document unavailable pane.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.220
Content is empty.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:02:00.070
Since that PDF was saved using Print and then
Save as PDF, it saved the text as an image,

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:02.880
so it is unreadable to assistive technology.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.830
Now, let’s hear a PDF that has readable
text but hasn’t been tagged, and the reading

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:09.250
order wasn’t checked.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:10.959
VoiceOver: California beaches.

00:02:10.959 --> 00:02:14.280
VoiceOver: When you think of California one
of the first things that comes to mind is

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:15.280
its beaches.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.170
Ride the wave and learn more about what beaches
are the most popular and how their temperatures

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:19.170
vary.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.500
VoiceOver: Most popular beaches temperatures.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:26.250
You'll notice the screen reader doesn't announce
the type of element like headings or graphics,

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:27.860
which takes away the structure.

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:30.170
I'm going to play the screen reader again.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:32.920
Take a look at what happens when these same
PDF gets to

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:33.950
the columns.

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:39.140
VoiceOver: According to U.S. News the top
three in the table below, the June 2021 California

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.840
beaches are temperatures compared for each
of the.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.170
VoiceOver: One.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:44.170
Two.

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:48.170
Because the PDF's reading order is wrong,
the screen reader reads the columns from left

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.820
to right instead of going column by column.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:54.760
Now, the PDF is tagged and has the reading
order corrected.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:03:00.210
Tagging the PDF and correcting the reading
order can be a tedious process the more complex

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:01.850
your documents get.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:03.270
Okay, let's hear the difference.

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:05.020
VoiceOver: Heading level 1, California beaches.

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:07.940
VoiceOver: When you think of California one
of the first things that comes to mind is

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:08.940
its beaches.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:11.970
Ride the wave and learn more about what beaches
are the most popular and how their temperatures

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:12.970
vary.

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:14.210
VoiceOver: Heading level 2, Most popular beaches

00:03:14.210 --> 00:03:18.270
VoiceOver: According to U.S. News, the top
three beaches are: 1.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:19.270
Laguna beach.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:20.270
2.

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:21.270
La Jolla.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:22.270
3.

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:23.270
Malibu beach.

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:24.270
VoiceOver: Malibu beach, graphic.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.270
Graphic.

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:26.270
VoiceOver: Heading level 2, Temperatures.

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:28.930
Now, the screen reader announces key structural
elements like headings, graphics with their

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:32.970
alternative text, and it's reading the columns
correctly.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:37.420
As you could tell, even pretty simple PDFs
quickly become inaccessible without tagging

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:38.959
and the right reading order.

00:03:38.959 --> 00:03:44.130
So, we suggest HTML webpages instead of PDFs
whenever possible.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:48.780
Here's why: First, Like we've said, PDFs take
extra work to make accessible.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:55.520
Second, It can be expensive to remediate complex
PDFs, and Third, PDFs can be hard for any

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.849
user to read on mobile.

00:03:57.849 --> 00:04:02.209
If you’ve opened a PDF on a mobile device,
you are probably familiar with the pinching

00:04:02.209 --> 00:04:07.720
to zoom and finger-scrolling needed to read
navigate PDFs on mobile.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:14.010
Remember, if a PDF is needed, it needs to
be properly tagged and have the right reading

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:18.160
order on top of all the other accessibility
design techniques.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:21.659
Otherwise, we suggest sticking to HTML web
pages.

